+# BLOCKED: two tests in `test_slug.py` require opposite behaviour
+
+`test_slug.py` cannot pass as written. Two of its tests call `slugify` with the
+*same input* and assert *different outputs*:
+
+| Test | Input | Expected |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `test_preserves_underscores` (line 22) | `"user_id field"` | `"user_id-field"` |
+| `test_underscores_become_dashes` (line 26) | `"user_id field"` | `"user-id-field"` |
+
+A function is deterministic: for one input it returns one value. Both
+assertions cannot hold simultaneously, so no implementation of `slugify` can
+make the suite green. The tests cite conflicting parts of the content team's
+spec (`4.2` "identifiers keep their underscores" vs `4.5` "slugs only ever
+contain `[a-z0-9-]`"), so the disagreement is in the spec itself, not just the
+test file. Per the request, the tests were left untouched.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+`slug.py` was implemented to satisfy every other requirement in the spec:
+lowercasing, punctuation removal, dash collapsing, accent folding, trimming.
+For underscores it follows **Spec 4.5** — the invariant that slugs only
+contain `[a-z0-9-]` — so underscores become dashes.
+
+Current result (6 of 7 passing):
+
+```
+$ python3 -m unittest test_slug
+...F...
+FAIL: test_preserves_underscores
+AssertionError: 'user-id-field' != 'user_id-field'
+Ran 7 tests ... FAILED (failures=1)
+```
+
+## To unblock
+
+The content team needs to decide which rule wins:
+
+1. **Underscores become dashes** — delete/rewrite `test_preserves_underscores`
+   (or change its expectation to `"user-id-field"`). No code change needed.
+2. **Underscores are preserved** — delete/rewrite
+   `test_underscores_become_dashes`, and in `slug.py` stop treating `_` as a
+   separator (remove `_` from the `[\s_]+` class and relax the `[a-z0-9-]`
+   invariant). Trivial follow-up once decided.
